UNAPPLIED wiring for fold-chat-answerspan.js (P2, 2026-10-07). Nothing here has been applied; the files are other lanes' (strand*, answerwire, index.html) and the tree has uncommitted work in them.
Made against the working tree as it was on 2026-10-07 (check with `patch -p1 --dry-run < eval/ants/p2/wire.diff` first; the hunks are small and anchored on unchanged lines).

WHAT IT DOES
 1. Sources-only mode (fold-chat-strand.js snipsOf): ONE answerSpan pass over the passages read gives the smallest span that answers. It becomes the first snip, kind "span":
      text     = the mechanical final pass (named rules, re-derivable)         e.g. "The tower is 330 metres (1,083 ft) tall."
      verbatim = the page's own bytes the span was cut from (a substring of the page text, or of the declared FAQ item)
      context  = the sentence it sits in (ONE TAP AWAY: the view's "the sentence" button)
    Every other snip of the turn is `more: true` and drawn behind "more from this page": the group / declared block is never the default view.
    verifySnip checks a span snip (the verbatim bytes occur in the page and the shown text is those bytes, or verifyRewrite re-derives it from them by the named rules).
    With no span the groups are drawn as before and the strand carries `minimalGap` = the typed gap ("no sentence in what was read states this"); the chat passes it to renderStrand(…, { gap }).
    An encyclopedia passage (tertiary) never supplies the span (it is a pointer, never a source).
    Off switch: STRAND.minimal = false (or snipsOf(…, { limits: { ...STRAND, minimal: false } })).
 2. THE SPAN IS NOT TRUSTED ALONE. Measured (eval/ants/P2-RESULTS.md): it holds the answer in ~70-84% of asks; its confidence separates right from wrong only weakly (AUC ~0.75 on the first held set).
    So the view opens the span WITH its sentence showing when confidence < 0.9 and no second, independent page carries the same figure/date/name (span.agreement >= 1); the
    group is always one tap away ("more from this page"). The strand alone contains the answer in ~69-74%; span or strand together ~86-94% (the "union" rows): that is what this wiring buys.
 3. The strand view (fold-chat-strandview.js + 4 lines of CSS in index.html): the span large, "the sentence" as a toggle, "more from this page" as <details>; the typed gap line when there is no span.
 4. The slot pipeline (fold-chat-answerwire.js): `minimalOfTurn(turn, question)` cuts the smallest span from the answer's own `row.sentence` (no outside sentence, so no pronoun is
    resolved from beyond it) and `runSlotTurn` attaches it as `turn.minimal`; `answerLine(turn)` returns it. contentAllowed (answerTurnBacksContent) accepts the content being either
    the realised line or that span (the every-word-occurs-in-row.sentence check still applies). The answer CARD (fold-chat-presentview.js) should draw `turn.minimal.text` first and
    `turn.minimal.sentence` behind the same "the sentence" tap; that file is not touched here.
 5. NOT wired: fold-chat.js needs no change (it already calls snipsOf and strandText); the stored message keeps `snips` (span fields verbatim/rewrite/context/agreement/more are stored by storeSnip).
    One integration fact: the strand's own junk gate (fold-chat-junk.js junkOf) calls a 5-word statement like "Ethanol boils at 78.37 °C" a fragment (< 6 words, no stop), so span snips are pushed
    after their own verification instead of through `add()`; answerSpan applies the chrome flags of junkOf to the shown span itself.

